There are many functions that are not documented here.  While many
DeadEnds functions are NOT described, the BULK of the DeadEnds
functions that are called by the port of LifeLines @b{ARE} documented.

The emphasis is on the core DeadEnds libraries.  The @code{LLSupport},
@code{Python}, and @code{UI} libraries are @b{NOT} considered core
DeadEnds libraries.  Descriptions of some of their functions and
variables are listed.  Others will be added in the future.

While the intent is to document @b{ALL} the functions meant to be
called by applications, which functions are in that category isn't
always clear.

In addition to functions meant to be called by applications, there are
functions meant only to be called from within the same library and
functions meant only to be called from within other DeadEnds
libraries.

The hope is that eventually there will be a collection of documents.
One for applications writers that want to use the DeadEnds libraries.
And another for developers that wish to modify the libraries --
whether to extend them or to fix bugs.  The latter, as yet unwritten,
document, will need to document not just the interfaces, but also the
internals.

It is hoped that this document will eventually encompass all of the
DeadEnds functions meant to be called directly by applications.

Additionally, as it is being written for the application developer,
not a library developer, its focus is on the @b{interfaces}, not on
the @b{internals}.  Documentation of the internals is important for
someone wishing to support, maintain, enhance, or extend the
libraries, but is not the focus of this document.  The focus of this
document is on @b{using} the libraries.  Hence the emphasis on the
interfaces.

The functions are grouped into three parts.

The first is those believed to be useful to applications.

The second is those which are considered internal to the libraries.
Many of these are non-static because they need to accessible to other
files in the library.  Some, because they need to be accessible to
other libraries.  Others, nobody noticed that they could be made
static.

The third part is those believed to be useful for debugging.  Whether
debugging an application or debugging the libraries.

For the functions which are documented that are @b{NOT} meant to be
called by applications, the hope is that they will eventually become
part of an internals document.

Currently, the header files make no distinction between functions and
variables for applications versus other functions and variables.  I
hope to change this.

Corrections, additions, and suggestions for improvement are always welcome.
